A constructor creates a fresh instance of a named struct. Its arguments
supply values for the fields in the declaration.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Point = |
        x I32,
        y I32 = 0,
    |

    first = Point(10)
    second = Point(x = 10, y = 20)
]

### Constructor arguments

Positional arguments fill fields in declaration order. After the first named
argument, name each remaining argument. Each argument must identify a field
and you can supply each field only once. A named argument can skip an earlier
field with a default.

Supply every field that lacks a default, including an optional field.
Omitting a defaulted field uses its default. Supplying `none` to an optional
field keeps it absent even if that field declares a default.

The declared field type checks each argument. In this example the fields
give the raw numeric literals their `I32` type. An already typed numeric
value must match the field type. For the shared notation, see
@../language-overview/#mon-syntax (Constructor syntax).

### Nested construction

When a field holds a named struct, use the child's constructor inside the
parent's argument list.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Size = | width I32, height I32 |
    Window = | size Size |

    window = Window(size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720))
]

The inner constructor supplies a `Size` to the outer constructor. An
anonymous record with matching field names can't fill that field. Nested
construction still uses separate, top-level type declarations.

### MON data

A MON reader can decode named literal entries into a struct because its
schema supplies the type and any omitted defaults. A qualifier such as
`Size` must match that schema. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).

### Field reads and writes

Use a dot to read a field. To write one, start from a mutable place and
prefix the whole field access with `~`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    point ~= Point(10, 20)
    ~point.x = 30
    horizontal = point.x
]

Reading a field takes shared access. Writing requires mutable access to the
root. The field's declared type stays the same in either case.

@../constants/#const-records (Const records) describes compile-time field
groups. @#anonymous-records (Runtime anonymous records) describes planned
local records with hidden types.
